// @test: run
// @level: L2
// @tier: 0
// @tags: modules, project, mount, stdlib, cross-file
// @timeout: 60000
// @project: stdlib_mount_in_sibling
// @expected-stdout: 1
// @description: A stdlib function mounted by a SIBLING module resolves.
//
//   The fixture's entry file mounts only `probe.reader`; the mount of
//   `core.encoding.json` lives in `src/reader.vr` and nowhere else. That
//   one placement was enough to break the program at run time (T1126):
//
//       main.vr does NOT mount core.encoding.json
//           -> Panic: [lenient] stage-5 qualified cross-module fn stub
//              never resolved (func_id=...)
//       main.vr ALSO mounts core.encoding.json
//           -> correct
//
//   and nothing else differed. `verum check` reported ZERO errors in both
//   cases, so the suite's compile-only specs could not have caught it —
//   this one has to RUN.
//
//   Why the failure hid for so long: builtin-type METHODS called from a
//   sibling module (`List.push`, `Text.len`) resolve through a different
//   path and always worked, and most module code is methods. A free
//   function mounted from the stdlib is the case nobody had written.
//
//   The carrier was a name harvester whose output feeds `wanted` — the set
//   that decides which archive functions are registered at all. It matched
//   five `ItemKind` variants and ended in `_ => {}`, so the `Module` variant
//   was swallowed silently, and with it every mount in every sibling file.
//   A missing arm there does not produce an error; it produces a SMALLER
//   SET, which no layer below can distinguish from a set that was never
//   asked for.
//
//   `stringify(json_int(7))` is "7" — one character. The number is small on
//   purpose: the spec is about whether the call ARRIVES, and a wide value
//   would invite reading it as a test of JSON.

fn main() {
    // The body lives in the fixture; this file carries the directives.
    // See stdlib_mount_in_sibling/src/{main,reader}.vr.
}
